+++ b/HEGEL_REPORT.md
@@ -0,0 +1,169 @@
+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report: lzma-rs
+
+Property-based tests written with [hegel](https://hegel.dev) (`hegeltest` 0.28.2,
+added as a dev-dependency with `--ignore-rust-version`; hegeltest's MSRV is 1.86
+vs. this crate's declared `rust-version = 1.71.0` — a maintainer decision if
+these tests are upstreamed). Shared generators live in `tests/common/mod.rs`;
+the tests themselves were added to the existing integration-test files
+`tests/lzma.rs`, `tests/lzma2.rs`, and `tests/xz.rs`.
+
+## Properties tested
+
+16 hegel tests covering 10 distinct properties. Payloads for round-trips come
+from a structured generator (`tests/common/mod.rs::draw_payload`) assembling
+runs of a single byte, repeated short patterns (match/distance encoding), ASCII
+text, and raw noise — not just incompressible noise. Evidence for each property
+is cited from the crate itself.
+
+### Round-trip identity (Tier-1 roundtrip)
+- `prop_lzma_round_trip_structured` (tests/lzma.rs)
+- `prop_lzma2_round_trip_structured` (tests/lzma2.rs)
+- `prop_xz_round_trip_structured` (tests/xz.rs)
+
+`decompress(compress(x)) == x` for each codec. Evidence: the crate's own
+`round_trip*` unit tests and the `fuzz/fuzz_targets/roundtrip_{lzma,lzma2,xz}.rs`
+fuzz targets assert exactly this.
+
+### Configuration generation: unpacked-size option pairs
+- `prop_lzma_round_trip_generated_unpacked_size_configs` (tests/lzma.rs)
+
+Draws one of the six compatible (encode `UnpackedSize`, decode `UnpackedSize`)
+pairs — `WriteToHeader(Some/None)` x `ReadFromHeader`/`ReadHeaderButUseProvided`,
+and `SkipWritingToHeader` x `UseProvided` — and asserts the round-trip for each.
+Evidence: the five `unpacked_size_*` unit tests each pin one concrete pair with
+one concrete payload; this generalizes them to arbitrary payloads and draws the
+configuration per the skill's "generate configurations, not just payloads".
+
+### Cross-implementation oracle
+- `prop_lzma_compress_interops_with_liblzma` (tests/lzma.rs)
+
+liblzma (via the `lzma` crate, already a dev-dependency used the same way in
+`assert_decomp_eq`) decodes `lzma_compress` output back to the original.
+An independent reference implementation that cannot share bugs with lzma-rs.
+
+### Decoder robustness on arbitrary bytes (Tier-1 parse robustness)
+- `prop_lzma_decompress_arbitrary_bytes_no_panic` (tests/lzma.rs)
+- `prop_lzma2_decompress_arbitrary_bytes_no_panic` (tests/lzma2.rs)
+- `prop_xz_decompress_arbitrary_bytes_no_panic` (tests/xz.rs)
+
+Decoding up to 4 KiB of arbitrary bytes returns a `Result`, never panics.
+Evidence: `fuzz/fuzz_targets/decompress_{lzma,lzma2,xz}.rs` are exactly this
+property; the maintainers consider it the contract.
+
+### Decoder robustness on corrupted valid streams
+- `prop_lzma_decompress_corrupted_stream_no_panic` (tests/lzma.rs)
+- `prop_lzma2_decompress_corrupted_stream_no_panic` (tests/lzma2.rs)
+- `prop_xz_decompress_corrupted_stream_no_panic` (tests/xz.rs)
+
+Compress a structured payload, then flip one bit, truncate at a drawn point, or
+overwrite one byte (`tests/common/mod.rs::corrupt`); decoding must not panic.
+Corruptions of *valid* streams reach much deeper decoder states than uniform
+garbage (which usually dies in the header). Evidence: same no-panic contract as
+above, plus the crate's `test_xz_block_check_crc32_invalid` corruption test.
+
+### Memory limit respected
+- `prop_lzma_memlimit_zero_rejects_nonempty_output` (tests/lzma.rs)
+
+With `memlimit: Some(0)`, decompressing any stream that produces at least one
+output byte fails with "exceeded memory limit of 0". Evidence: the `memlimit`
+unit test (one concrete payload) and the limit checks in
+`src/decode/lzbuffer.rs` (`append_literal` / `LzCircularBuffer::set`).
+
+### Provided-size metadata disagreeing with the stream
+- `prop_lzma_provided_size_takes_prefix_or_errors` (tests/lzma.rs)
+
+Decoding with `ReadHeaderButUseProvided(Some(m))`: if `m <= actual length`, the
+result is exactly the first `m` bytes of the original; if `m` is larger, the
+decoder errors (never panics). Evidence: `src/encode/options.rs` documents that
+a written size is unchecked against the input, so the decoder must cope with
+mismatches; behavior confirmed by probing before asserting.
+
+### Streaming == one-shot (feature = "stream")
+- `prop_lzma_stream_chunked_matches_one_shot` (tests/lzma.rs)
+
+Feeding the compressed stream to `decompress::Stream` in a drawn chunk size
+produces the same bytes as one-shot `lzma_decompress`. Evidence: the fixed
+`CHUNK_SIZES` loop in `assert_decomp_eq` and
+`fuzz/fuzz_targets/decompress_lzma_stream.rs`; the drawn chunk size explores
+sizes the fixed list misses.
+
+### Streaming robustness on arbitrary bytes (feature = "stream")
+- `prop_lzma_stream_arbitrary_bytes_no_panic` (tests/lzma.rs)
+
+Writing arbitrary bytes into `decompress::Stream` in arbitrary chunk sizes
+returns errors, never panics — including `finish()` after a failed write.
+Evidence: the stream state machine in `src/decode/stream.rs` explicitly handles
+the failed-write case ("previous write error").
+
+### `allow_incomplete` yields a prefix (feature = "stream")
+- `prop_lzma_stream_allow_incomplete_truncated_yields_prefix` (tests/lzma.rs)
+
+With `allow_incomplete: true`, a truncated valid stream can be `finish()`ed
+without the end-of-stream check, and the produced bytes are a prefix of the
+original data (finish may only fail while the 13-byte header + 5 range-coder
+start bytes are incomplete). Evidence: the `allow_incomplete` option docs in
+`src/decode/options.rs` and the `finish()` implementation in
+`src/decode/stream.rs`.
+
+## Test results
+
+- `cargo test`: 72 tests, all passing (32 unit + 22 lzma + 6 lzma2 + 11 xz + 1 doc target).
+- `cargo test --features stream`: 82 tests, all passing (includes the 3 stream-only properties and the stream branches of pre-existing tests).
+- Liveness check performed: inverting the assertion in
+  `prop_lzma2_round_trip_structured` produced a hegel failure with a fully
+  shrunk counterexample (empty payload, `draw_1 = 0`), then was reverted and
+  the `.hegel/` failure database removed.
+- Exploratory high-count run performed: all property tests run once at
+  `test_cases = 1000` (10x the default) in both feature configurations; no
+  failures. Committed tests use the default count.
+
+Environment note: building the pre-existing test suite requires liblzma dev
+headers for the `rust-lzma` dev-dependency (`liblzma-dev` was installed for
+this run).
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+None found. All 16 properties pass at the default and at 10x case count, in
+both the default and `stream` feature configurations. This is consistent with
+the crate being continuously fuzzed (the `fuzz/` directory covers the same
+robustness and round-trip surfaces with libFuzzer); the new tests bring those
+guarantees into `cargo test` with shrinking, and extend them to generated
+option configurations, corrupted-stream shapes, mismatched size metadata, and
+the `allow_incomplete` prefix behavior, which the fuzz targets do not cover.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+- **"Any corruption of a valid XZ stream causes a decode error"** — weakened to
+  no-panic. The byte-overwrite mutation can write the byte's existing value
+  (no-op), and asserting error-on-every-bit-flip would additionally rest on
+  every bit being covered by a checksum, which the crate does not promise.
+- **`SkipWritingToHeader` + `UseProvided(None)`** was excluded from the
+  generated configuration pairs: reading `src/encode/dumbencoder.rs::finish`
+  shows the end-of-payload marker is only written for `WriteToHeader(None)`,
+  so that pair has no terminator and is not a supported combination (the
+  crate's own unit tests never pair them either).
+- **"Corrupted stream decodes to output no longer than the original"** —
+  considered and dropped: a bit flip in the 8-byte unpacked-size field can
+  legitimately declare a larger size, and LZMA match ops can legally expand
+  output well beyond input size; the crate makes no such promise.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+- The Rust reference was accurate throughout: the MSRV `cargo add` failure and
+  its `--ignore-rust-version` fix, the `hegeltest` package vs `hegel` crate
+  naming, `.hegel/` gitignore advice, and the "passing tests print nothing —
+  break one to verify liveness" workflow all matched reality exactly.
+- The "generate configurations, not just payloads" section and the tip that
+  `fuzz/` targets show what maintainers consider worth generating were the two
+  most useful prompts for this crate; the fuzz directory directly suggested
+  half the properties.
+- Helper functions taking `&hegel::TestCase` (per the reference) worked well
+  for sharing payload/corruption generators across three integration-test
+  targets via `tests/common/mod.rs`.
+- One gap: no guidance on codebases whose *pre-existing* dev-dependencies fail
+  to build (here `rust-lzma` needing system liblzma headers). A note in the
+  Setup/Gotchas section about getting the existing suite green before adding
+  hegel would have saved a diagnosis step.
+- A `sampled_from`-over-enum example for drawing one of N config pairs would be
+  handy; drawing a small integer and matching on it works but is less
+  self-documenting.
